# Scalability Strategy

Defines how the template stays maintainable as more features, platforms, and teams are added.

## Mandatory rules

- Must keep features independent and feature-first.
- Must keep cross-cutting services in `core` only when multiple features use them.
- Shared UI must stay generic and business-free; all reusable components must live under `frontend/lib/shared/`.
- Public feature exports must be intentional.
- Must avoid global mutable state.
- Must use clear contracts between layers.
- Should document architecture decisions that affect future development.
- Must not copy whole screens to support new layouts.
- UI must be fully responsive across mobile, tablet, and desktop breakpoints.
- Must use theme tokens exclusively; no hard-coded style tokens.

## Scaling patterns

| Problem | Required pattern |
|---|---|
| many features | feature-first modules |
| many screens | route groups and shell routes |
| many data records | pagination, streams, local indexes |
| many UI variants | theme tokens and component composition |
| many developers | rules, linting, tests, PR checklist |

## Acceptance checklist

- Adding a feature does not require modifying unrelated features.
- Shared components do not become product-specific dumping grounds.
- Architecture boundaries remain enforceable by review and tests.

